# Push_Swap

Project from the 42 curriculum.

## Description

Push_Swap sorts integers (positive and negative) in ascending order using two stacks (a and b) and a limited set of operations.  
The goal is to sort the numbers using the fewest possible moves, which are printed to standard output.

## Operations

sa / sb - swap the first two elements  
ss - sa and sb at the same time  
pa / pb - push the top element between stacks  
ra / rb - rotate upwards  
rr - ra and rb together  
rra / rrb - reverse rotate  

## Compilation

make

## Usage

./push_swap 3 2 1  
./push_swap "3 2 1"

## Tests

Test with 500 numbers and checker:

ARG="$(shuf -i 0-499 | tr '\n' ' ')"  
./push_swap $ARG | ./checker_linux $ARG

Test with Valgrind:

ARG="$(shuf -i 0-499 | tr '\n' ' ')"  
valgrind ./push_swap $ARG | ./checker_linux $ARG

Count moves:

ARG="$(shuf -i 0-499 | tr '\n' ' ')"  
./push_swap $ARG | wc -l

## Algorithm

Uses the Turk algorithm, chosen for being simpler and more readable than bit-based approaches.
